  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nWednesday night through Thursday night\nAssessment...low (<25%) confidence on rain chances\n\nMoisture transport into the area is very weak to non-existent in the \nlow levels. Thus mainly dry conditions will be seen with the \npotential for some isolated sprinkles or showers. The current\nmodel consensus of higher rain chances is likely overdone.\n\nFriday through Tuesday\nAssessment...medium (40-60%) confidence on late week rain potential.\nMedium to high (60-80%) confidence on the chances of a significant\nsystem next week.\n\nFriday into Saturday a respectable surge of moisture will be \ntransported into the area. This moisture is a combination of the \nremnants from Vance and the Gulf opening up. Just going on the \nprogged arrival of the moisture, Friday night into Saturday would \nhave the better chances for rain. The model consensus shows \nthis with rain chances running 40-60% depending upon the \nlocation.\n\nSeveral ensemble members show different timing for this moisture. \nThus the model consensus has a 20-40% chance of rain Saturday night \ninto Sunday. The various model solutions and their ensembles should \nbegin to converge to a better timing over the next few days.\n\nNext week has the potential to be interesting.\n\nThe various global models show a weakening of the 10 hPa mean zonal \nwinds at 60 N suggesting the potential for a pattern change. So far \nthe global models only show a temporary pattern change which is the \nresult of all models developing a significant mid-latitude cyclone.\n\nThe models agree on the development of a large mid-latitude \ncyclone early next week. However, there are differences on where\nthe system develops/evolves. These differences determine on how\nstrong the system could become and where it eventually tracks.\n\nThe initial surge of moisture comes from a plume off the Pacific \nwhich helps develop the system. As the system develops, the Gulf \nopens up supplying even more moisture. The development area for\nthe system ranges from the central to southern Plains.\n\nOnce developed, there is loose agreement between the global models \nthat a sub 1000mb system develops, especially if phasing of the\nnorthern stream occurs. If phasing occurs, the system deepens\nand eventually occluding as it slowly moves east or east \nnortheast. Thus the potential exists for a widespread rain if \nthe system passes near or just south of the area. Such a track \nwould also create windy conditions as the system strengthens.\n\nRight now the model consensus is targeting the Monday/Tuesday time \nframe for the best rain chances. I would not be surprised if the \nbetter rain chances are delayed 12-24 hours depending upon \nwhen/where the system develops.",
